From: ours (wine-mac-builds), 2026-11-15
Upstream: no (WineHQ master still uses virtual_rect here, checked 2026-11-15)
Why: when a program changes the display mode under EmulateModeset, win32u
 creates the window's GL client surface "raw" and renders into a
 framebuffer surface (an FBO of the emulated, virtual size) that it blits
 to the client surface at the raw monitor size (monitor_rect). winemac
 ignores client.raw and sets the CGL surface backing size to virtual_rect,
 so the blit lands in a backing of the wrong size and the picture is
 zoomed or cropped. winex11 already uses
 "raw ? monitor_rect : virtual_rect" for the same thing; do the same.
 Only surfaces created raw change; everything else keeps virtual_rect.
 Seen in White Wolf (Gcenx 11.21, our macfixes 11.21 build1): the log
 shows "Using experimental framebuffer OpenGL surface" and the picture
 overflows the window. Wine 10.0 has no framebuffer surface, so this is
 11.x only.
Status: not yet built or tested.
Applies to: wine 11.21 (macfixes, after 0050)

diff --git a/dlls/winemac.drv/opengl.c b/dlls/winemac.drv/opengl.c
index a67010a..e5e50dc 100644
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/opengl.c
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/opengl.c
@@ -1149,7 +1149,7 @@ static void macdrv_context_select_drawable(struct macdrv_context *context, struc
     if (drawable && drawable->client)
     {
         struct macdrv_client_surface *surface = impl_from_client_surface(drawable->client);
-        RECT rect = surface->client.virtual_rect;
+        RECT rect = surface->client.raw ? surface->client.monitor_rect : surface->client.virtual_rect;
         macdrv_make_context_current(context->context, surface->cocoa_view, cgrect_from_rect(rect));
         return;
     }
